ns, and I'd expect the second to cause most of the eight seconds. I only have the one line of SQL. There's no schema or index definition in the repo, so I'm inferring the rest and haven't run `EXPLAIN`.\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM orders o JOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id WHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\n**Scans the query forces**\n\n1. **Full scan of `users`.** Wrapping the column in `lower(u.email)` stops a plain index on `email` from being used. The database has to evaluate `lower()` on every user row. This one grows with your user count.\n2. **Likely full scan of `orders`.** The join goes through `o.user_id`. Without an index on that column, the database either scans all of `orders` or hashes it to find the matching user's rows. If a large table is the cause of the 8 seconds, this is the one.\n3. **Wide rows.** `SELECT *` returns every column of both tables, including `users` columns the orders page probably doesn't show. It also rules out index-only scans.\n\n**Fix** (Postgres syntax; adjust if you're on another database)\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n```\n\nThen select only the columns the page needs:\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, o.status  -- whatever the page renders\nFROM orders o\nJOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\nIf you'd rather not keep an expression index, store emails already lowercased or use `citext`. Then the query can compare `u.email = ...` directly against a normal index.\n\n**Why it got slow last week:** the file can't tell me. The likely causes are an index dropped or never created on `orders.user_id`, the `lower()` wrapper being added recently, or `orders` growing past the point where a scan was tolerable. `git log -p -- query.sql` would show whether the query changed. Running `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on it will show which scan dominates, and I'd do that before creating the indexes. If you can share the plan or the schema, I can be more specific.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}